From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bootowser <b@o.local>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 20:41:02 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Bootowser] add the bootowser-control sidecar for page-driven
 commands

The kiosk browser is the device's control surface: the page the operator
deploys needs to run shell commands and scripts on the machine it runs on
(volume, relays, restarting services, installing media). Firefox gives web
pages no such API, and granting one from inside the browser process would
be the wrong boundary twice over: bootowser.service runs with
NoNewPrivileges=true and ProtectSystem=strict, so a command spawned there
could neither escalate nor touch the system, and a renderer compromise
would share an address space with the executor.

So the executor is its own process, built from this tree like pingsender:

  * toolkit/bootowser-control: a small loopback-only HTTP/1.1 daemon,
    GeckoProgram with linkage=None (no libxul: the command channel
    survives a browser restart) and DisableStlWrapping for the same
    reason pingsender does it. It rides in the package manifest beside
    pingsender, so release packages carry it and not just dist/bin.
  * POST /v1/exec runs an arbitrary shell string as the kiosk user via
    sh -c in its own process group with a fresh minimal environment;
    timeout kills the whole group (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL), stdout/stderr
    are captured up to a byte cap.
  * POST /v1/root runs a named script from a root-owned commands
    directory through sudo -n; the shipped sudoers rule grants exactly
    that directory, and every script is re-validated (root-owned, not
    group/world writable, regular file) before each run.
  * /v1/* is gated on an Origin allowlist derived from the operator's
    START_URL (preflight included), with an X-Bootowser-Token fallback
    for curl-style callers that send no Origin. GET / serves a minimal
    same-origin admin console.
  * Everything is configurable through the environment so the unit can
    supply /etc/bootowser/control.conf via EnvironmentFile=; non-loopback
    binds are refused unless explicitly overridden.
  * Each request is audited to stderr (journal): origin, target, exit
    status, duration, and the command or script name with control
    characters stripped.
